WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.580
ROBERT MARTIN: So I'm
Robert Martin from Google,

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.830
and I'm joined by
my friend Simon

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.900
Protheroe from Square Enix.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.400
And today, we're
going to be talking

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:16.000
about unlocking the value
of data for your games.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.600
So the first half
of the talk is going

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.240
to be primarily
around architecture

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.960
and around simplification
and scaling of analytics

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.540
for large global games.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.820
And then the second
part of the presentation

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.850
will be Simon talking about
some of the really incredible

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.850
analysis that Square has
been doing around several

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.530
of their games.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:42.870
So who went to the Stadia
announcement yesterday?

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.760
Or who watched it online?

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.410
Everybody excited about that?

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.420
We're very excited to
be adding another logo

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.360
up there to all the awesome
consumer services that Google

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:52.230
is launching.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:56.270
And we're obviously most
excited about gaming.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:59.420
So Google Cloud, in particular--

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.580
we view ourselves
as the hub that

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.680
joins a lot of those
other services together.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.800
It helps you integrate
them and bring value

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.920
across all of those
different Google offerings.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.220
And we have four pillars that
we're very passionate about.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.010
Number one is Cross
Platform and Cross Play.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.350
And the way that
we accomplish that

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:21.110
is by abstracting away
a lot of the technology

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:24.500
so that you can focus on
games and data and analytics

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.330
and ultimately,
customer experiences.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:30.268
We also are very committed to
open source and open standards,

00:01:30.268 --> 00:01:31.685
and you'll see
some of that today.

00:01:31.685 --> 00:01:33.410
And that's pretty
different than what

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:38.000
you see in gaming solutions
that you can find elsewhere.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.220
ML &amp; Data Analytics--
obviously a strong area

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.960
of expertise for Google.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.930
And we'll tap into that a
little bit this afternoon.

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:48.382
And I think that first slide
did a good job of laying out

00:01:48.382 --> 00:01:50.840
that one Google picture, and
all the different ways that we

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:53.430
can help you.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.490
So machine learning-- one
of the really interesting

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.410
challenges about
machine learning.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.720
Everyone wants to take
their games into this realm

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.385
so that they can provide more
customized experiences so

00:02:04.385 --> 00:02:07.260
that they can more effectively
monetize their games.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:10.668
But it's really challenging
to unlock the insights

00:02:10.668 --> 00:02:12.210
from your data with
machine learning,

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:15.060
because there just aren't
that many data scientists.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.160
There's 21 million
of us developers,

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.770
but there's only about a million
data scientists worldwide,

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.930
and there's only about
several thousand deep

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:23.250
learning researchers.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.350
And most of them
probably work for Google.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.320
So when you're thinking
about unlocking insights

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.020
with machine learning, you
need to get the infrastructure

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.320
and the technology out of the
way of the data scientists,

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.900
and you need to bring developers
into that type of analysis.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:43.330
And so if you think about
traditional data and analytics,

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:47.500
a majority of the time is really
spent supporting, scaling,

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.720
and tuning the
infrastructure that's

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.660
necessary to process
data at large scale.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:56.200
So you actually spend a minority
of your team's time focused

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.670
on insights.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:00.400
And at Google, we ran into
the same problem years ago,

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.470
actually, as we
tried to scale up.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.140
And we wanted to flip
that equation on its head

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:08.200
and have our developers and our
data scientists spending most

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:11.230
of their time on analysis
and insights, and a minority

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:14.043
of time really thinking
about infrastructure.

00:03:14.043 --> 00:03:15.460
That'll inform a
lot of what we're

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.790
going to talk about here today.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:21.500
So this is Google's journey
through big data processing,

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:25.130
all the way back to about
2002-2004 time frame,

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:27.750
where we invented
distributed file systems.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:30.210
And we also invented
the MapReduce algorithm.

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.210
So that, for us,
allowed us to unlock

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:35.150
all the consumer-level
data that we

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:38.780
were collecting with all of
our consumer facing products.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:43.700
But we very quickly outgrew
MapReduce's ability to scale.

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:46.760
And so in 2008, we invented
a data processing engine

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.950
called Dremel that greatly
altered the path as far

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:54.860
as how we built a data
processing pipeline.

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:57.390
And we're going to
dig into that today.

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:59.570
So if you look at that
center lane on the slide,

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:02.720
that's essentially
Google's journey internally

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:05.750
to modernize its data
processing capabilities.

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:08.940
But along the way, I mentioned
we're committed to open source.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.330
So we've been surfacing a
lot of that software for you

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:12.880
as open source APIs.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:16.550
So things like Hadoop,
HBase, Apache Drill, and even

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:17.954
TensorFlow.

00:04:17.954 --> 00:04:19.579
Now, the really
exciting part, I think,

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:21.500
about what we're doing
today is that we're

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:24.830
surfacing not only the
software as open source,

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:27.320
but all of that infrastructure
that we're using internally

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.450
to power it as Google Cloud.

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:34.060
And so we'll talk about
that a little bit later.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:37.230
So what does a data
pipeline look like on GCP?

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:38.850
What do we mean when
we've abstracted

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:40.770
all the infrastructure away?

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:42.480
So if you've done
data processing

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.820
in a Hadoop-based
ecosystem, you're

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.260
familiar with a lot of
different programming models,

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:48.960
a lot of different
tools, and a lot

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:52.320
of different layered
solutions to try and achieve

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.990
real-time performance and scale.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:57.210
When you abstract the
infrastructure away

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:00.010
and you write the solution
from the beginning for scale,

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:01.920
it becomes a very
simple picture.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:05.250
So our data pipeline has
three primary components.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:07.440
If you want to stream
data from a game,

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.530
you broadcast to
our global message

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:12.330
bus, which we call Pub/Sub.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:15.540
Think of it as a global
serverless Kafka.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:17.080
Then you need to take that data.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:19.560
You need to transform
it into a shape that's

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.190
suitable for storage.

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:23.130
And that's what
we call Dataflow.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:26.130
And Dataflow takes your
Java or Python code

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:28.440
and scales it up
as high as it needs

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.410
to go to process the incoming
messages from that data

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:32.400
pipeline.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.550
And then the final
storage area is BigQuery,

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.530
and that's our serverless
data warehouse.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:40.260
And that's what we're going to
actually focus on most today.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:43.980
So three very simple components
that all scale and all require

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:47.710
zero infrastructure management.

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:50.040
So this is what the reference
architecture for a gaming

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.735
solution looks like built
on that data pipeline.

00:05:52.735 --> 00:05:54.360
So at the center of
the picture, you've

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:57.300
got BigQuery, which is just
a data warehouse with tables

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:01.530
and views, like any that
you're used to using with SQL.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:03.660
The primary difference
is how it can scale,

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:05.670
and we'll talk about
that in just a minute.

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:07.830
Up at the top, you've
got your game clients

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:10.050
and your game servers
that are streaming data

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:11.370
over to the right.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.350
And we're ingesting
that data with Pub/Sub,

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:17.970
transforming it with Dataflow,
and storing it into BigQuery.

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:20.650
Now, you also need to do
batch ingestion in many cases.

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:21.870
You might have log files.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:23.640
You might have chat
transcripts that you

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:26.400
want to drop off of your
servers or your clients.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.980
And you can pull that in
in a batch methodology

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:32.040
through cloud storage,
also using Dataflow.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:34.595
So whether you're streaming
or you're batching your data,

00:06:34.595 --> 00:06:35.970
you're using the
same programming

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:41.100
model in both cases, and
the same scaling model.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.700
So let's dig into BigQuery
a little bit more deeply.

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:45.950
This is where things get
a little bit interesting.

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:49.940
So if you're familiar with
traditional architectures

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:53.110
for processing data, you
have a collection of nodes,

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:55.760
and each node is generally
responsible for compute

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:57.440
and for storage.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:01.250
With BigQuery, what we did is
we realized that couldn't scale.

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:03.980
Because sometimes, you have
massive quantities of data

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:06.380
that you want to store
cost efficiently.

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:09.260
And other times, you have
extremely complex queries

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:10.430
that you want to run.

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:12.980
And those two things don't
always scale together.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:16.075
And in the traditional model,
you had to scale them together.

00:07:16.075 --> 00:07:17.450
So the first thing
we did when we

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:20.000
designed Dremel,
which became BigQuery,

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:23.700
is we separated
compute from storage.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:26.640
Now, you might say you just
broke the fundamental rule

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.670
of data processing--
keep data and compute

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:31.720
close to each other.

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:34.860
And so we couldn't just
separate compute and storage.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:36.690
We had to connect
them back together

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:39.370
with an extremely fast network.

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:42.540
So we actually have a
petabit scale network

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:45.540
that sits in between our
compute and storage which

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:48.800
allows us to shuffle
data extremely quickly.

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:51.700
And we'll talk more about
that in just a minute.

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:53.550
So there's some pretty
exotic technology

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:55.110
that's powering BigQuery.

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:57.120
But the interface to
it is the same SQL

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:59.790
that you can use in
MySQL, or SQL Server,

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:03.400
or Postgres-- whatever
you're used to.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:07.410
So we also store the data
in an interesting way.

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:09.270
So BigQuery is a
relational database.

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:11.550
Generally in a
relational database,

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:14.220
rows and columns of data
are stored together.

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:16.230
Because we have that
petabit network,

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:18.480
we can take the columns
in your database

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:20.310
and break them apart as well.

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:22.830
And that allows us to do some
interesting things as far

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:27.420
as how we process the data
and how we optimize the data.

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:30.240
So we take the best
of NoSQL databases

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:33.600
and apply that architecture
to a relational database.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.610
And that petabit
network allows us

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:38.640
to take columns from one
table, or from multiple tables

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:43.429
that we're joining, and put
them back together very quickly.

00:08:43.429 --> 00:08:46.310
So we process a lot of
sensitive data at Google.

00:08:46.310 --> 00:08:49.820
Think of your Gmail
documents, and Drive

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:52.850
documents, and Sheets, and
all the metadata associated

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:53.660
with that.

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:57.470
We would never store your data
without high-grade encryption.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:59.990
And so in BigQuery, that
same high-grade encryption

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:01.400
is available to you.

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:03.050
Each one of those
columns is actually

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:04.700
encrypted with a
separate encryption

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:10.220
key, which is, in response, also
encrypted with a key encryption

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:10.840
key.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:13.040
And we rotate all of
those keys for you.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:14.750
So the encryption
piece is there,

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:16.430
but it's also
abstracted as well,

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:19.150
so you don't have
to worry about it.

00:09:19.150 --> 00:09:22.190
So we've talked about the
architecture of BigQuery.

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:24.750
You might be saying to yourself,
this sounds pretty cool,

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:28.320
but it sounds a lot like other
things that are out there.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:30.920
One of the things that I want
to really communicate today

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:33.365
is the scale of BigQuery.

00:09:33.365 --> 00:09:34.740
So when you think
about BigQuery,

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:38.180
this is not just a cluster
that's running in a data center

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:41.090
BigQuery is the
data center itself.

00:09:41.090 --> 00:09:43.970
Our data centers are built
around the capability

00:09:43.970 --> 00:09:46.260
to deliver data at scale.

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:48.860
So when you create a
dataset in BigQuery,

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:50.480
there are actually
three facilities

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:53.060
like this that are
managing all of the data

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:54.110
that you're storing.

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:57.050
Your data is replicated
automatically to all three

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:58.587
of those facilities.

00:09:58.587 --> 00:10:00.920
Well, you might look at that
picture and say, all right,

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:01.420
Robert.

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:04.610
So there's still just a cluster
running in there, right?

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:07.730
It's a big building, but there's
a cluster, and that's BigQuery.

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:09.800
I want to give you
a specific example

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:11.630
of what I mean
when I say we build

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:14.810
our facilities around BigQuery.

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:17.570
So remember I mentioned
that petabit network?

00:10:17.570 --> 00:10:21.780
A petabit is a million times
faster than a gigabit network.

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:24.170
How did Google create
a network that's

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:29.310
a million times faster than what
you generally see out there?

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:30.890
This is how.

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:37.430
So to build a petabit network,
we took 100,000 10-gig E links

00:10:37.430 --> 00:10:39.980
and combined them into a fabric.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:42.620
So this picture shows
you a small piece

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:46.100
of that fabric that's in each
one of those three facilities.

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:47.870
This piece of
equipment is the length

00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:51.090
of a football field in each
one of those facilities.

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:55.280
That's what allows us to shuffle
data back and forth at scale

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:56.860
on petabyte-size datasets.

00:10:59.865 --> 00:11:01.740
One other thing I want
to talk about briefly,

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:03.870
and then we're actually
going to get hands on here

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:06.780
and take a look at what
BigQuery can do live.

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:09.450
I want to talk a little
bit about machine learning.

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:12.510
So not only has Google
built purpose-built hardware

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:15.330
to store and process
your data, we also

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:17.370
have built
purpose-built hardware

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:20.940
to build, train, and run
inference on your machine

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:22.500
learning models.

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:25.030
Now again, why did we do this?

00:11:25.030 --> 00:11:26.430
So there are GPUs out there.

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:28.620
We have GPUs in
our data centers.

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:33.690
Why go and build custom silicon
to train and run predictions?

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:34.620
The reason is simple.

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:36.060
Again, it's scale.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:39.900
We need to be able to
constantly iterate.

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:43.272
So if you've ever used something
like Smart Reply in Gmail,

00:11:43.272 --> 00:11:44.730
you might notice
that it just seems

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:46.818
to get better every few weeks.

00:11:46.818 --> 00:11:49.110
And that's because we're
allowed to-- we can constantly

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:52.620
iterate and retrain our models,
because the silicon that's

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:55.110
built specifically
for machine learning

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:57.850
is 15 to 30 times faster.

00:11:57.850 --> 00:11:59.460
So faster is good.

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:01.560
But also remember that
most of our products

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:04.050
that are consumer
facing are free.

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:06.520
So it also needs to
be cost effective.

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:10.290
So we get 30 to 80 times
the operations per watt

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:12.300
as we would get on a GPU.

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:15.492
That means less power
consumption and less heat.

00:12:15.492 --> 00:12:16.450
Now, how do we do that?

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:19.720
How is it that this
chip is so much faster?

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:22.780
Well, if you think
about a GPU, a GPU

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:24.720
is designed to
perform one vector

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:27.222
operation per clock cycle.

00:12:27.222 --> 00:12:29.430
If you think about training
a machine learning model,

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:31.420
you're working on matrices.

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:34.410
So if you want to do a
matrix operation on a GPU,

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:38.290
you have to iterate across
every row of that matrix.

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:41.370
So that's multiple clock
cycles to effectively perform

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:42.900
one operation.

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:46.920
Our tensor processor units
are matrix processing, so

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:49.890
each tick of the clock, we
can process an entire matrix

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:51.060
operation.

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:53.280
So that's how we're
able to train faster

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:55.750
and more cost-effectively.

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:57.750
And so now I'd like to
jump into the console,

00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:01.920
and we'll take a quick look at
what BigQuery can do at scale.

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:10.300
So I just happen to have a
petabit of data laying around,

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:13.630
and I thought we might run
a couple of SQL queries

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:16.450
on a petabyte while I'm up here.

00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:22.030
So this dataset
is 1.09 petabytes.

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:26.170
We have a trillion rows
in this table, stored.

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:28.520
And we're going to
run a SQL query on it.

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:30.607
So the first thing I
want to do is, in case

00:13:30.607 --> 00:13:32.440
you haven't worked with
BigQuery, let's take

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:34.270
a quick look at the structure.

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:36.700
So BigQuery is just like
any relational database.

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:38.895
You have tables
that have columns.

00:13:38.895 --> 00:13:40.270
So we have a set
of columns here,

00:13:40.270 --> 00:13:42.760
just like you'd have in MySQL.

00:13:42.760 --> 00:13:46.870
We've got familiar ANZDATA
types here, so very familiar.

00:13:46.870 --> 00:13:49.330
When we talk about
simplifying the interface

00:13:49.330 --> 00:13:51.340
and empowering your
data scientists,

00:13:51.340 --> 00:13:52.360
this is what we mean.

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:55.460
This looks like a
typical SQL database.

00:13:55.460 --> 00:13:57.860
So let's take a quick
look at the data.

00:13:57.860 --> 00:13:59.590
So this is a preview
here, but you

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:01.600
can see that the
data is arranged

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:03.370
in exactly the same
way that it would

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:06.310
be arranged in any database
that your data scientists have

00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:07.580
been using.

00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:09.670
So we're providing a
really simple interface

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:13.855
to some very powerful
infrastructure on the back end.

00:14:13.855 --> 00:14:14.480
So that's cool.

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:17.930
That's a quick preview of what
a table looks like in BigQuery.

00:14:17.930 --> 00:14:20.930
Let's go ahead and take a
look at what running a query

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:22.260
looks like.

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:25.790
Let's see if I can zoom
in here a little bit.

00:14:25.790 --> 00:14:28.580
You can see this
query a little better.

00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:31.250
All right, so we're going
to do a SELECT query.

00:14:31.250 --> 00:14:35.090
We're going to do a SELECT query
on the sales partition table.

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:37.220
Again, it's a trillion rows.

00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:39.770
We're going to round and
sum one of the columns.

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:41.510
We're going to do a
group by, so we're

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:43.093
going to be
aggregating the data.

00:14:43.093 --> 00:14:45.260
And we're also going to be
sorting all of that data,

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:47.120
on all trillion rows.

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:49.730
We're also going to be doing a
search for a specific customer

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:50.450
key.

00:14:50.450 --> 00:14:52.910
And because we don't require
you to do things like indexes

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:56.210
in BigQuery-- in fact, they
don't even exist in BigQuery--

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:59.360
we're going to do a full
table scan on a trillion rows

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:01.070
when I run this query.

00:15:01.070 --> 00:15:03.430
So I'm going to zoom
out, and let's go ahead

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:04.510
and kick this query off.

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:07.410
So I just say "run query."

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:09.840
And our query is going
to start processing.

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:12.820
Now, let me give you an idea
of what's happening here.

00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:15.870
So we're going to go and select
those two tablets for those two

00:15:15.870 --> 00:15:18.692
columns that I'm querying,
pull them out of storage,

00:15:18.692 --> 00:15:21.150
and we're going to shuffle them
across that petabit network

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:22.445
to the compute nodes.

00:15:22.445 --> 00:15:24.570
The first thing the compute
nodes are going to do--

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:26.970
it's got two tablets
that it has to decrypt--

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:28.920
all trillion rows in each one.

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:31.950
Then we're going to decompress
the data at about 4 to 1.

00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:34.170
So the data is going to
get about four times as big

00:15:34.170 --> 00:15:35.792
as it was in storage.

00:15:35.792 --> 00:15:37.250
And then our worker
nodes are going

00:15:37.250 --> 00:15:38.917
to look at the
instructions in the query

00:15:38.917 --> 00:15:41.100
and begin executing those.

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:43.710
And so that's what's
happening seamlessly

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:46.230
as we're processing this query.

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:49.110
If I was to do a SELECT
star on this same table,

00:15:49.110 --> 00:15:51.100
it would run in about
the same amount of time,

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:53.770
because we just scale the
workers out horizontally.

00:15:53.770 --> 00:15:56.083
And that's happening
automatically.

00:15:56.083 --> 00:15:57.750
So this isn't going
to take long to run,

00:15:57.750 --> 00:15:59.850
but I'm a little
bit impatient, and I

00:15:59.850 --> 00:16:02.650
like to spend my time
doing productive things.

00:16:02.650 --> 00:16:04.440
So let's jump over
and do something else

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:06.060
while we're waiting.

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:09.210
So I'm going to show you
BigQuery's ability to train

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:12.570
machine learning models in SQL.

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:14.340
So if you can see
this query, this

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:16.510
is another dataset that I have.

00:16:16.510 --> 00:16:18.648
And this contains
birth data for babies.

00:16:18.648 --> 00:16:20.190
And we're going to
build a model that

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:22.530
will allow us to predict
the birth weight of babies

00:16:22.530 --> 00:16:25.200
based on some characteristics
about those births.

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:27.860
Now, this could be
useful for a game,

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:31.140
but you could just as easily
use the same technique

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:34.860
to train a player lifetime
value model or a player toxicity

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:35.520
model--

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:37.027
same techniques.

00:16:37.027 --> 00:16:38.110
So let's take a look here.

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:39.735
The first thing I'm
going to do is just

00:16:39.735 --> 00:16:42.660
a standard query to pull
the attributes that I

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:45.360
want to train my model
on, as well as the label

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:46.798
that I'm trying to train.

00:16:46.798 --> 00:16:48.840
So I'm going to pull a
few items about the birth,

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:50.423
and then I'm going
to pull the weight.

00:16:50.423 --> 00:16:51.870
Standard query.

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:54.300
Now, to train a
machine learning model,

00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:56.880
I have a bit of
additional SQL up here

00:16:56.880 --> 00:16:59.130
that says take the
output of that query

00:16:59.130 --> 00:17:02.280
and feed it into a
linear regression.

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:05.819
So let's go ahead and
kick off a training job

00:17:05.819 --> 00:17:07.349
on this linear regression model.

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:13.290
And so again, the same
workflow is happening here.

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:16.380
BigQuery goes out, calls
the data up from storage,

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:18.900
shuffles it across
that petabit network.

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:21.390
And now, instead of
performing query operations,

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:24.420
those exact same workers
are adjusting weights

00:17:24.420 --> 00:17:27.089
on all of those input
variables to optimize

00:17:27.089 --> 00:17:30.980
the performance of that
linear regression model.

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:33.610
And this should finish up
here in just a few seconds.

00:17:33.610 --> 00:17:34.620
OK, so we're done.

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:37.140
27 seconds-- we trained
a machine learning model

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:39.370
on four gigs of data.

00:17:39.370 --> 00:17:40.380
So that's step one--

00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:41.910
training the model.

00:17:41.910 --> 00:17:43.830
Step two for your
data scientists

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:46.260
is evaluating the model, right?

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:49.200
So we need to see how well
that iteration of our model

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:50.070
performed.

00:17:50.070 --> 00:17:51.750
Maybe we need some
additional input

00:17:51.750 --> 00:17:56.350
values to be able to
accurately predict baby weight.

00:17:56.350 --> 00:18:00.100
So again, I'm going to run
another vanilla SQL query here.

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:03.810
Let's pull up all of the values
we fed into the training model,

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:07.230
but now with a bit of additional
SQL up here at the top.

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:10.590
Let's ask BigQuery to
evaluate our model performance

00:18:10.590 --> 00:18:13.440
on our predicted baby weights
versus the ones that are

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:14.870
stored in our training dataset.

00:18:20.595 --> 00:18:24.905
This will just take
a few seconds here.

00:18:24.905 --> 00:18:25.982
OK.

00:18:25.982 --> 00:18:27.440
So now what I'm
going to show you--

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:29.390
we get some nice
statistics back.

00:18:29.390 --> 00:18:31.785
So our data scientists
want mean squared error,

00:18:31.785 --> 00:18:33.410
and they want r
squared, because that's

00:18:33.410 --> 00:18:36.620
what tells them how well
this model is performing.

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:38.945
So in this case, we've got
an r squared of about 5%.

00:18:38.945 --> 00:18:40.730
We've got some work
left to do to bring

00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:43.160
in some additional data
points so that we get really

00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:44.810
nice, accurate baby weights.

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:47.330
But I've got the information
that I need immediately

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:49.560
to make those adjustments.

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:52.580
So the final step in training
our machine learning model

00:18:52.580 --> 00:18:55.050
is running predictions.

00:18:55.050 --> 00:18:58.460
So to run predictions with
our fully-trained model now,

00:18:58.460 --> 00:19:01.760
we run a SQL query that simply
pulls up the input values

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:04.280
to the model, and
our predict function

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:07.182
will return our
predicted baby weights.

00:19:07.182 --> 00:19:08.390
So let's run that real quick.

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:15.980
And this should only
take a few seconds here.

00:19:15.980 --> 00:19:20.040
OK, so we processed four gigs
of data there-- five seconds.

00:19:20.040 --> 00:19:25.190
And we returned 220,000
predicted baby weights.

00:19:25.190 --> 00:19:28.370
So in the amount of time that
it took for me to start up

00:19:28.370 --> 00:19:30.930
a SELECT query on
a trillion rows,

00:19:30.930 --> 00:19:33.620
we were able to train,
evaluate, and run predictions

00:19:33.620 --> 00:19:35.210
on a machine learning model.

00:19:35.210 --> 00:19:37.040
So this is what we
mean about getting out

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:38.870
of the way-- getting
the infrastructure out

00:19:38.870 --> 00:19:43.220
of the way of your very talented
and very scarce and in-demand

00:19:43.220 --> 00:19:44.760
folks on your team.

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:48.770
Let's go check back in on
our trillion row table scan.

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:50.210
And it's done.

00:19:50.210 --> 00:19:52.520
It ran in about two
and 1/2 minutes.

00:19:52.520 --> 00:19:56.510
We processed 32 terrabytes
of data on those two columns.

00:19:56.510 --> 00:19:59.960
And here are our total
sales, aggregated by day

00:19:59.960 --> 00:20:01.190
and sorted for us.

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:08.400
What do you think?

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:08.970
Not bad.

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:10.720
That would take a
pretty big Spark cluster

00:20:10.720 --> 00:20:11.880
to be able to do that.

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:14.590
You probably wouldn't want
to pay the bill for it.

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:17.280
So let's jump back
over to our slides,

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:21.870
and I'd like to close
by talking a little bit

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:25.073
about a customer that has
taken the full journey.

00:20:25.073 --> 00:20:25.740
And that's King.

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:29.100
We actually have published
a whitepaper with them.

00:20:29.100 --> 00:20:32.220
They have taken full
advantage at scale

00:20:32.220 --> 00:20:34.150
of this infrastructure.

00:20:34.150 --> 00:20:35.700
And what I mean
"at scale," they're

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:39.190
processing 50 billion
events per day.

00:20:39.190 --> 00:20:41.460
So you might be
saying to yourself,

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:44.280
that scale is amazing,
but we're not there yet.

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:47.730
What I would say to you is,
everyone needs simplicity.

00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:50.520
Who would want to have to
spend their time managing

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:53.400
infrastructure if
they didn't have to?

00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:56.370
And number two, scale is coming.

00:20:56.370 --> 00:20:59.640
So mobile and console
are converging.

00:20:59.640 --> 00:21:03.510
The scale of mobile data
is coming to console.

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:05.250
And we also have hyper-casual.

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:08.040
It's a new, emergent
genre which generates

00:21:08.040 --> 00:21:10.480
massive quantities of data.

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:12.880
So you need the
simplicity today.

00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:15.120
And even if you don't
have the scale today,

00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:16.760
tomorrow the scale
will be there.

00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:19.000
And it's coming
faster than you think.

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:22.500
So at this point, I'd
love to introduce Simon

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:24.000
from Square Enix.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:28.188
And he will talk about some of
the really fascinating analyses

00:21:28.188 --> 00:21:30.480
that they're doing from the
business side on their data

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:32.010
with BigQuery.

00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:32.886
Thank you.

00:21:32.886 --> 00:21:33.608
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:33.608 --> 00:21:35.316
SIMON PROTHEROE: Do
you have the clicker?

00:21:35.316 --> 00:21:35.802
ROBERT MARTIN: There you go.

00:21:35.802 --> 00:21:37.135
SIMON PROTHEROE: It's over here?

00:21:37.135 --> 00:21:38.560
Thank you.

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:39.060
Great.

00:21:39.060 --> 00:21:39.935
Well, thanks, Robert.

00:21:39.935 --> 00:21:41.670
And thanks to Google
for the opportunity

00:21:41.670 --> 00:21:43.128
to tell you a little
bit about what

00:21:43.128 --> 00:21:46.530
we've been doing at Square Enix
for the last couple of years.

00:21:46.530 --> 00:21:48.030
Hopefully, Square
Enix doesn't need

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:51.360
much of an introduction
for a GDC audience.

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:53.170
In case you're new
to the industry,

00:21:53.170 --> 00:21:56.280
we're one of the leading
developers and publishers

00:21:56.280 --> 00:21:57.930
of games across the world.

00:21:57.930 --> 00:22:01.440
We're known most for
big AAA franchises,

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:04.350
such as "Final Fantasy," "Dragon
Quest," "Kingdom Hearts," "Tomb

00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:06.150
Raider," "Space Invaders."

00:22:06.150 --> 00:22:09.090
But we actually have
a very broad portfolio

00:22:09.090 --> 00:22:11.820
of games on pretty much
any gaming platform

00:22:11.820 --> 00:22:14.530
and any business model.

00:22:14.530 --> 00:22:18.600
And that's something I want to
talk a little bit about today.

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:20.250
A little bit about me.

00:22:20.250 --> 00:22:22.860
I joined Square in--

00:22:22.860 --> 00:22:27.120
well, I joined Eidos as
a small startup in 1993.

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:29.730
And at the time,
we were six guys

00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:31.920
in a boathouse on
the River Thames,

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:35.312
about a mile upstream from Henry
VIII's palace at Hampton Court.

00:22:35.312 --> 00:22:37.020
Things have obviously
changed quite a bit

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:38.805
in the last 25 years.

00:22:38.805 --> 00:22:41.430
And I've spent most of that time
in a number of different roles

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:43.380
within first Eidos,
and then after it

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:48.990
was bought by Square Enix, in
a number of different roles,

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:52.740
really looking at the boundary
between business and technology

00:22:52.740 --> 00:22:55.350
development, and where
new technology enables

00:22:55.350 --> 00:22:58.570
us to grow our business into
new markets, new platforms,

00:22:58.570 --> 00:23:00.670
new business models, and so on.

00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:02.400
And that's how we
end up today, looking

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:04.530
at what we're
doing with big data

00:23:04.530 --> 00:23:07.893
and how we're addressing
that kind of challenge

00:23:07.893 --> 00:23:09.810
I happen to be the guy
that's standing up here

00:23:09.810 --> 00:23:10.750
talking about this.

00:23:10.750 --> 00:23:12.630
There is, if not a
cast of thousands,

00:23:12.630 --> 00:23:15.000
a cast of dozens
of people behind me

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:17.040
that have contributed
