00:23:40.740 --> 00:23:42.880
I'll try and mention
those as we go through.

00:23:42.880 --> 00:23:45.180
But anybody I forget,
then my huge apologies,

00:23:45.180 --> 00:23:48.400
and I'll buy you a beer later.

00:23:48.400 --> 00:23:51.270
So if we look at what
we're doing with big data,

00:23:51.270 --> 00:23:52.770
we've got a number
of challenges,

00:23:52.770 --> 00:23:55.140
both business and technical.

00:23:55.140 --> 00:23:58.890
Both Square Enix and
Eidos have been collecting

00:23:58.890 --> 00:24:02.310
data in a very determined
way for about 10 years,

00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:05.700
since before Square
bought Eidos.

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:07.920
And although we've
been collecting

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:11.940
data in a concerted
way, it hasn't

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:14.290
been in a particularly
organized way.

00:24:14.290 --> 00:24:16.830
So what we've got
is-- or what we had--

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:20.110
is a lot of silos of
data of different kinds.

00:24:20.110 --> 00:24:23.070
We had a lot of game
telemetry, but that tended

00:24:23.070 --> 00:24:26.942
to be grouped into
different types of games--

00:24:26.942 --> 00:24:28.650
games developed in
Japan, games developed

00:24:28.650 --> 00:24:30.780
in the West, games
using one technology

00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:32.940
suite versus another.

00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:35.550
Some games, the game
data was entirely

00:24:35.550 --> 00:24:36.690
held within the game team.

00:24:36.690 --> 00:24:38.640
Others it was centralized.

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:40.537
But really quite fragmented.

00:24:40.537 --> 00:24:42.120
And then we had a
lot of other sources

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:44.130
of data around our customers--

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:47.170
web analytics data, showing
their interaction with us

00:24:47.170 --> 00:24:49.420
across a range of different
websites-- the Square Enix

00:24:49.420 --> 00:24:53.040
website, but also individual
game sites, and so on.

00:24:53.040 --> 00:24:55.950
Any interactivity
with our CRM system.

00:24:55.950 --> 00:24:58.530
If you click on an
email or open an email,

00:24:58.530 --> 00:25:00.450
that data is logged
and tracked, but again,

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:02.200
in a separate system.

00:25:02.200 --> 00:25:05.790
Similarly, purchase data,
social media interactions,

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:07.260
registration data--

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:10.640
a whole bunch of
different sources of data.

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:13.877
And they were all stored across
a number of different teams,

00:25:13.877 --> 00:25:15.960
by different people, using
different technologies.

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:18.780
Some of it, for example,
was in a third-party system

00:25:18.780 --> 00:25:20.640
like Google Analytics.

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:23.190
In some cases, it was in
a SQL Server database.

00:25:23.190 --> 00:25:26.124
In others, Oracle,
and others, MySQL.

00:25:26.124 --> 00:25:29.028
Sometimes it was sitting
under some guy's desk.

00:25:29.028 --> 00:25:30.570
Other times, it was
in a data center.

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:33.300
But it was really a
pretty fragmented picture.

00:25:33.300 --> 00:25:36.560
And that led to a number
of technical challenges.

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:39.200
So about five, six years ago,
we started trying to pull this

00:25:39.200 --> 00:25:42.650
data together-- at least, the
game telemetry side of it--

00:25:42.650 --> 00:25:47.600
and build what was at the time
a state-of-the-art solution

00:25:47.600 --> 00:25:50.480
for being able to
analyze that data.

00:25:50.480 --> 00:25:53.580
We had a large on--
well, not large, large.

00:25:53.580 --> 00:25:57.020
But a, for us, large on-prem
Hadoop cluster in a data

00:25:57.020 --> 00:25:59.300
center in Montreal.

00:25:59.300 --> 00:26:02.800
It was using licensed
software, licensed per node.

00:26:02.800 --> 00:26:05.750
And that gave us a challenge
that we didn't really have

00:26:05.750 --> 00:26:08.105
the scalability that we wanted.

00:26:08.105 --> 00:26:11.540
To increase the capacity
of that kind of cluster

00:26:11.540 --> 00:26:13.610
meant adding in a whole node.

00:26:13.610 --> 00:26:16.790
And that was roughly
$20,000 or so.

00:26:16.790 --> 00:26:18.590
So it wasn't a trivial
business decision

00:26:18.590 --> 00:26:20.960
to add a node to that cluster.

00:26:20.960 --> 00:26:23.180
And that was pretty much a
one-way scaling operation,

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:23.680
as well.

00:26:23.680 --> 00:26:26.180
Once we scaled up,
we were committed.

00:26:26.180 --> 00:26:27.540
We bought the hardware.

00:26:27.540 --> 00:26:29.525
It was an annual license
fee for the software.

00:26:29.525 --> 00:26:31.400
So we weren't really
able to scale back down.

00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:33.230
So if we weren't
convinced we were

00:26:33.230 --> 00:26:36.947
going to use that hardware
for more than a short term,

00:26:36.947 --> 00:26:38.780
then it was very difficult
to justify making

00:26:38.780 --> 00:26:41.490
the investment in scaling.

00:26:41.490 --> 00:26:43.240
In parallel with that--
so we were running

00:26:43.240 --> 00:26:44.350
at-- most of the
time, we were running

00:26:44.350 --> 00:26:47.020
at capacity with whatever the
capacity of that cluster was.

00:26:47.020 --> 00:26:48.437
Occasionally we
would scale it up,

00:26:48.437 --> 00:26:49.780
but we were running at capacity.

00:26:49.780 --> 00:26:51.363
And it meant the
data engineering team

00:26:51.363 --> 00:26:54.700
spent all their time
spinning plates or juggling

00:26:54.700 --> 00:26:57.010
fiery chainsaws or something
like that to try and keep

00:26:57.010 --> 00:26:58.540
things from falling over.

00:26:58.540 --> 00:27:01.210
They were optimizing the way
in which the data was stored,

00:27:01.210 --> 00:27:04.433
making sure that we
were evicting old data.

00:27:04.433 --> 00:27:06.850
And the problem was that they
were spending all their time

00:27:06.850 --> 00:27:08.800
managing that cluster
and managing the data,

00:27:08.800 --> 00:27:11.300
rather than actually do anything
that was genuinely of value

00:27:11.300 --> 00:27:13.050
to the business.

00:27:13.050 --> 00:27:15.540
And also, that demo
from Robert a minute ago

00:27:15.540 --> 00:27:17.790
was fascinating, because we
just couldn't do something

00:27:17.790 --> 00:27:18.630
like that.

00:27:18.630 --> 00:27:22.170
We weren't able to--
we had data scientists.

00:27:22.170 --> 00:27:24.990
Again, very talented guys,
who were really keen to

00:27:24.990 --> 00:27:27.712
do a lot of experimentation
with machine learning models.

00:27:27.712 --> 00:27:28.920
But we just couldn't do that.

00:27:28.920 --> 00:27:31.980
We couldn't take a
large volume of data

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:34.800
and throw 1,000 calls of
CPU at it over 48 hours

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:37.560
to do some massive training
job, because we just

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:40.520
didn't have the resources.

00:27:40.520 --> 00:27:46.660
So what we wanted to do was
to completely reboot our data

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:48.123
handling infrastructure.

00:27:48.123 --> 00:27:49.540
A colleague of
mine, Tim Ward, who

00:27:49.540 --> 00:27:51.693
heads up the Analytics
and Insights team,

00:27:51.693 --> 00:27:53.860
spent a lot of time going
around the whole business,

00:27:53.860 --> 00:27:57.610
speaking to pretty
much every stakeholder

00:27:57.610 --> 00:28:01.035
from all the teams across the
business, all the locations,

00:28:01.035 --> 00:28:02.942
at various levels
of the organization.

00:28:02.942 --> 00:28:04.900
And we put together a
pretty comprehensive list

00:28:04.900 --> 00:28:06.190
of requirements.

00:28:06.190 --> 00:28:07.870
And a few themes
came out of that.

00:28:07.870 --> 00:28:10.930
The overall top vision that
we're aiming for is what we

00:28:10.930 --> 00:28:12.190
call a "single-gamer view."

00:28:12.190 --> 00:28:14.680
By analogy with
retail FMCG companies

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:16.480
that have
single-customer views, we

00:28:16.480 --> 00:28:18.220
wanted a single-gamer
view that gave us

00:28:18.220 --> 00:28:20.890
a holistic view of a
gamer's interaction

00:28:20.890 --> 00:28:24.430
with us across everything
that we could capture.

00:28:24.430 --> 00:28:29.530
So gameplay data on a
range of different formats,

00:28:29.530 --> 00:28:32.660
commercial data, marketing data,
social media, and everything.

00:28:32.660 --> 00:28:34.660
But pulling all of that
together and represented

00:28:34.660 --> 00:28:37.150
by a single identity
so that we knew

00:28:37.150 --> 00:28:39.550
that this person on Facebook
was that person on our email

00:28:39.550 --> 00:28:40.050
database.

00:28:40.050 --> 00:28:42.670
This player on Xbox
Live, that guy on Steam,

00:28:42.670 --> 00:28:45.210
this guy on Nintendo Switch.

00:28:45.210 --> 00:28:46.805
So pulling all of
that data together.

00:28:46.805 --> 00:28:48.680
And there are a few
other lower level things.

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:50.510
So the first is this.

00:28:50.510 --> 00:28:53.360
The big driver for this was
that we needed it to be scalable

00:28:53.360 --> 00:28:54.890
in a cost-effective
way, to enable

00:28:54.890 --> 00:28:56.870
us to do that business
experimentation that

00:28:56.870 --> 00:28:59.680
was difficult for us before.

00:28:59.680 --> 00:29:02.823
We also, given the lifecycle
of a game and the way

00:29:02.823 --> 00:29:04.240
in which our game
teams worked, we

00:29:04.240 --> 00:29:06.490
wanted this to be a very
flexible solution-- something

00:29:06.490 --> 00:29:09.070
that we weren't
setting in stone today

00:29:09.070 --> 00:29:12.820
how that data has to look for
the next three, five years.

00:29:12.820 --> 00:29:16.920
During development, game
schema will often change.

00:29:16.920 --> 00:29:20.090
The types of data that you
want to collect changes.

00:29:20.090 --> 00:29:24.730
We'd also potentially add
additional sources of data.

00:29:24.730 --> 00:29:26.440
As the game is
released, and you've

00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:29.820
got DLC drops, and continue
the evolution of the game,

00:29:29.820 --> 00:29:32.110
you will, again, want
to be able to flex

00:29:32.110 --> 00:29:33.990
the data that you're capturing.

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:36.640
And that goes for other
sources of capture, as well.

00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:38.710
So we wanted to be able
to augment that data

00:29:38.710 --> 00:29:40.990
and to process it on
the fly without having

00:29:40.990 --> 00:29:44.780
to tear down pipelines and
rebuild them and so on.

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:47.560
And finally, pulling
all of that data

00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:50.350
together represents a
huge opportunity for us

00:29:50.350 --> 00:29:51.790
from a business perspective.

00:29:51.790 --> 00:29:54.670
But it's also obviously a
pretty significant risk.

00:29:54.670 --> 00:29:56.920
The more data we're
bringing together,

00:29:56.920 --> 00:29:58.690
the more we're
exposing ourselves

00:29:58.690 --> 00:30:02.290
to the risks of data
which was previously

00:30:02.290 --> 00:30:05.260
anonymized becoming PII,
because we're connecting it

00:30:05.260 --> 00:30:06.705
all together.

00:30:06.705 --> 00:30:08.580
And it represents a
pretty high-value target.

00:30:08.580 --> 00:30:11.860
So we now need to make sure that
we had the internal protections

00:30:11.860 --> 00:30:14.530
and controls around that
data, and governance policies

00:30:14.530 --> 00:30:16.360
around it to keep
our data protection

00:30:16.360 --> 00:30:20.730
officer happy and smiling.

00:30:20.730 --> 00:30:22.770
So spoiler alert.

00:30:22.770 --> 00:30:24.840
We ended up partnering
with Google Cloud.

00:30:24.840 --> 00:30:27.460
Which, can I have that water?

00:30:27.460 --> 00:30:29.940
Excuse me.

00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:30.880
No big surprise there.

00:30:30.880 --> 00:30:32.576
But it wasn't a
foregone conclusion.

00:30:35.730 --> 00:30:38.800
Once we'd gathered all
those requirements,

00:30:38.800 --> 00:30:42.930
we then spent some time with
all the major public cloud

00:30:42.930 --> 00:30:47.040
providers, and a number of more
bespoke specialist companies

00:30:47.040 --> 00:30:48.900
that offered solutions--

00:30:48.900 --> 00:30:50.970
in some cases tailored
to games, in some cases

00:30:50.970 --> 00:30:53.968
more single-customer
view type solutions.

00:30:53.968 --> 00:30:54.760
We brought them in.

00:30:54.760 --> 00:30:55.275
We sat them down.

00:30:55.275 --> 00:30:57.233
We showed them our problem,
talked a little bit

00:30:57.233 --> 00:30:59.490
about the current
challenges we faced,

00:30:59.490 --> 00:31:02.900
and asked them what
they could do to help.

00:31:02.900 --> 00:31:05.560
And we had a number of
different approaches.

00:31:05.560 --> 00:31:08.617
Some of them were, we
can build this for you.

00:31:08.617 --> 00:31:11.200
We've got a professional service
organization full of experts,

00:31:11.200 --> 00:31:13.033
and we can just give
you a solution to this.

00:31:13.033 --> 00:31:14.880
And that's explicitly
not what we wanted.

00:31:17.290 --> 00:31:18.790
We ended up going
with Google Cloud,

00:31:18.790 --> 00:31:21.940
to cut a long story short,
for a number of reasons.

00:31:21.940 --> 00:31:23.580
As Robert's pointed
out, the technology

00:31:23.580 --> 00:31:26.590
is stunningly excellent.

00:31:26.590 --> 00:31:29.080
On the network side,
for example, we--

00:31:29.080 --> 00:31:32.650
as part of-- we build
proofs of concept

00:31:32.650 --> 00:31:36.608
on a number of those platforms.

00:31:36.608 --> 00:31:38.900
Obviously, there's no point
in doing a proof of concept

00:31:38.900 --> 00:31:40.898
for scalability if
you're just working

00:31:40.898 --> 00:31:43.440
with the amount of data you can
copy around in a spreadsheet.

00:31:43.440 --> 00:31:45.770
So we were using not
petabytes of data,

00:31:45.770 --> 00:31:49.860
but tens of terabytes
of data as a test set.

00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:55.140
And getting that data into
the vendors' architecture

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:58.278
and moving it around was
lightning-fast with Google.

00:31:58.278 --> 00:31:59.820
It was really not
a challenge at all.

00:31:59.820 --> 00:32:01.470
It took a couple of
hours to upload the data.

00:32:01.470 --> 00:32:03.660
And then once it's in GCP,
it's pretty much instant

00:32:03.660 --> 00:32:05.400
to move around.

00:32:05.400 --> 00:32:09.390
With some other vendors, it
took many, many hours, sometimes

00:32:09.390 --> 00:32:11.030
even a small number of days.

00:32:11.030 --> 00:32:13.290
And in some cases,
we never got there.

00:32:13.290 --> 00:32:18.300
So just the fundamental
basics of the architecture

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:20.770
knocked us out of the
park from day one.

00:32:20.770 --> 00:32:23.430
The other thing is
that for us, Google

00:32:23.430 --> 00:32:25.875
felt like a good cultural fit.

00:32:25.875 --> 00:32:28.500
And I think that's probably come
into a lot of games companies.

00:32:28.500 --> 00:32:31.980
We're completely happy with
guys turning up in t-shirts

00:32:31.980 --> 00:32:34.890
and just talking
tech from day one.

00:32:34.890 --> 00:32:36.450
We're actually less
happy with people

00:32:36.450 --> 00:32:38.220
turning up in smart
suits and giving us

00:32:38.220 --> 00:32:40.140
polished sales presentations.

00:32:40.140 --> 00:32:42.010
Not true for every industry.

00:32:42.010 --> 00:32:46.830
But for us, the people that
we met at Google, from account

00:32:46.830 --> 00:32:50.250
management through tech support
through solutions engineering

00:32:50.250 --> 00:32:54.420
and so on, felt like a
good match for the teams

00:32:54.420 --> 00:32:56.910
that we had, and the kind of
people that we had on our side

00:32:56.910 --> 00:32:59.200
working on this project.

00:32:59.200 --> 00:33:02.030
And finally, it
felt as though this

00:33:02.030 --> 00:33:06.230
was something that they were
going to help us to build,

00:33:06.230 --> 00:33:08.690
rather than something they were
going to build and sell us.

00:33:08.690 --> 00:33:11.720
And that's a really
important point.

00:33:11.720 --> 00:33:16.250
Of course, the fundamental thing
that we build is great games,

00:33:16.250 --> 00:33:17.180
and that's--

00:33:17.180 --> 00:33:20.480
our business lives or dies
by making fantastic games,

00:33:20.480 --> 00:33:21.890
obviously.

00:33:21.890 --> 00:33:24.950
But that is becoming--

00:33:24.950 --> 00:33:28.810
over time, that is
becoming to be not enough.

00:33:28.810 --> 00:33:32.210
We need to have a
transformational approach

00:33:32.210 --> 00:33:34.730
to the way we handle data.

00:33:34.730 --> 00:33:37.640
And this represented a big
strategic platform for us

00:33:37.640 --> 00:33:38.400
to do that.

00:33:38.400 --> 00:33:40.330
And so it was really
important that we felt

00:33:40.330 --> 00:33:41.580
that we had ownership of that.

00:33:41.580 --> 00:33:44.260
We've got some
phenomenal engineers

00:33:44.260 --> 00:33:45.830
on the team who worked on this.

00:33:45.830 --> 00:33:47.663
A couple of them are
here today, but there's

00:33:47.663 --> 00:33:51.770
plenty of others back
in London, LA, Montreal,

00:33:51.770 --> 00:33:53.660
who have worked
on this platform.

00:33:53.660 --> 00:33:57.783
And we wanted to feel that those
guys owned this platform, that

00:33:57.783 --> 00:33:59.450
understood it, and
that we weren't going

00:33:59.450 --> 00:34:02.620
to be relying on third-party,
external companies

00:34:02.620 --> 00:34:04.370
every time we needed
changes or something.

00:34:04.370 --> 00:34:06.710
We needed to feel that
this was something that we

00:34:06.710 --> 00:34:09.489
had control of from day one.

00:34:09.489 --> 00:34:15.580
And the other point is that
the Google platform in general

00:34:15.580 --> 00:34:17.949
felt very integrated.

00:34:17.949 --> 00:34:20.900
There are other providers.

00:34:20.900 --> 00:34:23.380
Some of the platforms felt
a little bit disjointed.

00:34:23.380 --> 00:34:25.210
With Google, we
didn't feel that.

00:34:25.210 --> 00:34:26.900
That was actually a
bit of a surprise.

00:34:26.900 --> 00:34:30.562
The engineering team
originally, I think,

00:34:30.562 --> 00:34:32.020
were a little bit
concerned when we

00:34:32.020 --> 00:34:36.670
started looking initially at the
different providers' offerings.

00:34:36.670 --> 00:34:38.780
The feeling was, well,
GCP doesn't really

00:34:38.780 --> 00:34:40.495
have enough sliders and knobs.

00:34:40.495 --> 00:34:41.620
We can't really control it.

00:34:41.620 --> 00:34:44.110
It's a bit of a black box.

00:34:44.110 --> 00:34:47.530
What we came to realize
during those initial concept

00:34:47.530 --> 00:34:50.330
phases was that actually,
that's the strength.

00:34:50.330 --> 00:34:53.020
And a huge amount of work has
gone on behind the scenes,

00:34:53.020 --> 00:34:56.260
as Robert alluded to earlier, to
make sure that from our point,

00:34:56.260 --> 00:34:59.030
it is a black box that
just kind of works.

00:34:59.030 --> 00:35:02.470
So we've got-- the
controls that are offered

00:35:02.470 --> 00:35:04.870
to you as a developer
are the ones you need,

00:35:04.870 --> 00:35:07.660
rather than just all the ones
that they could make available.

00:35:07.660 --> 00:35:09.988
And that's been a tremendous
advantage over time.

00:35:09.988 --> 00:35:11.530
There have been a
couple of occasions

00:35:11.530 --> 00:35:14.110
where it hasn't
quite been enough.

00:35:14.110 --> 00:35:17.920
So for example, the previous
version of Dataflow,

00:35:17.920 --> 00:35:20.710
which is based on
Apache Beam One,

00:35:20.710 --> 00:35:23.950
didn't allow us to
migrate schema on the fly,

00:35:23.950 --> 00:35:27.160
so we couldn't-- as data
came in, if the data came

00:35:27.160 --> 00:35:29.680
in wasn't matching the
schema in BigQuery,

00:35:29.680 --> 00:35:34.660
we basically had to restart
the pipeline and rebuild.

00:35:34.660 --> 00:35:36.580
We worked with Google on that.

00:35:36.580 --> 00:35:38.710
Explained the problem, they
came up with solutions,

00:35:38.710 --> 00:35:43.930
and we found that we had a
solution when Dataflow moved

00:35:43.930 --> 00:35:47.410
to Beam Two that will allow
us to migrate data on the fly

00:35:47.410 --> 00:35:49.010
without restarting everything.

00:35:49.010 --> 00:35:50.947
So there are occasions
where it's not

00:35:50.947 --> 00:35:52.030
quite everything you need.

00:35:52.030 --> 00:35:54.460
But there, that
engineering-led culture

00:35:54.460 --> 00:35:58.360
really made the difference in of
getting us over those hurdles.

00:35:58.360 --> 00:36:03.240
But in general, having things
as a closed box that just works

00:36:03.240 --> 00:36:04.870
has been tremendously useful.

00:36:04.870 --> 00:36:06.740
Somebody once made the analogy--

00:36:06.740 --> 00:36:08.590
one of the Google sales team--

00:36:08.590 --> 00:36:11.020
that it's a little bit like
Apple consumer products.

00:36:11.020 --> 00:36:14.530
You think about the iPod when
it first came out, compared

00:36:14.530 --> 00:36:16.270
to MP3 players at the time.

00:36:16.270 --> 00:36:18.670
Lots of them had lots of
menus and screens and so on.

00:36:18.670 --> 00:36:21.087
And suddenly, Apple comes out
with something with a screen

00:36:21.087 --> 00:36:22.420
and little wheel and that's it.

00:36:22.420 --> 00:36:26.870
And it turns out to be the most
awesome consumer experience.

00:36:26.870 --> 00:36:28.550
And that's the right thing.

00:36:28.550 --> 00:36:30.460
So I think there's
an awful lot of work

00:36:30.460 --> 00:36:32.290
that it's easy to
overlook that goes on

00:36:32.290 --> 00:36:36.250
behind the scenes on the
GCP side to make sure

00:36:36.250 --> 00:36:38.500
that from a consumer's
perspective,

00:36:38.500 --> 00:36:40.960
a consumer of the
engineering services,

00:36:40.960 --> 00:36:43.860
that it's what you need.

00:36:43.860 --> 00:36:46.790
So to cut a long story short,
we partnered with Google.

00:36:46.790 --> 00:36:50.660
We, using their
reference architecture,

00:36:50.660 --> 00:36:53.450
built a solution which
looks very similar to that.

00:36:53.450 --> 00:36:54.302
This is ours.

00:36:54.302 --> 00:36:56.510
If you remember the slide
that Robert put up earlier,

00:36:56.510 --> 00:36:58.160
this is a very similar slide.

00:36:58.160 --> 00:37:00.710
We've got live streaming
data coming in from games

00:37:00.710 --> 00:37:02.630
and some other sources.

00:37:02.630 --> 00:37:06.110
We have some on-premise,
some other data

00:37:06.110 --> 00:37:09.680
that's coming in in Batch.

00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:12.200
So in some cases, that's
some games that are still

00:37:12.200 --> 00:37:14.620
sending overnight play logs.

00:37:14.620 --> 00:37:16.120
We've got other
sources of data that

00:37:16.120 --> 00:37:21.380
are ingested daily, sometimes
even less frequently than that.

00:37:21.380 --> 00:37:22.790
That data is coming in.

00:37:22.790 --> 00:37:25.040
The batch data goes
straight, basically,

00:37:25.040 --> 00:37:29.630
into storage straight through
Dataflow and into BigQuery.

00:37:29.630 --> 00:37:31.970
Streaming data is
published a Pub/Sub,

00:37:31.970 --> 00:37:34.520
and then Dataflow subscribes
to that and captures the data

00:37:34.520 --> 00:37:36.230
and puts it into BigQuery.

00:37:36.230 --> 00:37:39.500
On both sides, Dataflow is
doing the data validation,

00:37:39.500 --> 00:37:43.455
augmentation,
sanitization, and so on.

00:37:43.455 --> 00:37:45.080
I'll come onto a
couple of other things

00:37:45.080 --> 00:37:47.200
where we use Dataflow
as well, which

00:37:47.200 --> 00:37:49.970
have been nice additional
benefits that have really

00:37:49.970 --> 00:37:52.580
helped us out of a hole
with some other problems.

00:37:52.580 --> 00:37:56.450
And then within
BigQuery, the data

00:37:56.450 --> 00:37:59.580
is controls, in terms of
access, at a project level.

00:37:59.580 --> 00:38:01.670
So we have a number of projects.

00:38:01.670 --> 00:38:05.180
We have two projects,
actually, per game,

00:38:05.180 --> 00:38:07.385
with different users
assigned to each project.

00:38:07.385 --> 00:38:09.260
And then there are other
projects, and things

00:38:09.260 --> 00:38:11.540
like sales data and
general datasets

00:38:11.540 --> 00:38:13.330
that go across everything.

00:38:13.330 --> 00:38:15.770
And we're able to control
at a quantifying level

00:38:15.770 --> 00:38:19.870
who has access to what data
and what they need it for.

00:38:19.870 --> 00:38:24.300
Similarly, on the visualization
and reporting side,

00:38:24.300 --> 00:38:26.417
obviously we've got BigQuery
in the middle there.

00:38:26.417 --> 00:38:28.000
We've got a number
of different users.

00:38:28.000 --> 00:38:31.000
We have general business users--
people who basically just need

00:38:31.000 --> 00:38:32.320
to CKPI dashboards.

00:38:32.320 --> 00:38:34.510
May be able to drill it
in, or compare this month

00:38:34.510 --> 00:38:36.530
with last month or something.

00:38:36.530 --> 00:38:39.250
But they're looking at
dashboard-level stuff.

00:38:39.250 --> 00:38:41.500
We have been using
Power BI for that.

00:38:41.500 --> 00:38:44.710
These days, we're using Data
Studio for that much more.

00:38:44.710 --> 00:38:49.372
When we first started working on
this project, Data Studio was--

00:38:49.372 --> 00:38:50.830
quite frankly, it
was wasn't really

00:38:50.830 --> 00:38:52.690
there compared to Power BI.

00:38:52.690 --> 00:38:55.810
It has caught up very
rapidly, and we're

00:38:55.810 --> 00:38:57.360
now very comfortable
with Data Studio

00:38:57.360 --> 00:39:00.308
as a kind of visualization
tool at the dashboarding level.

00:39:00.308 --> 00:39:02.725
Similarly, for Power users who
need to slice and dice data

00:39:02.725 --> 00:39:05.500
a bit more, we
were using Tableau.

00:39:05.500 --> 00:39:07.840
We're now using Looker,
another Google partner which

00:39:07.840 --> 00:39:11.335
allows people to basically
do the same sort of things

00:39:11.335 --> 00:39:13.960
they would use in Tableau to be
able to slice and dice the data

00:39:13.960 --> 00:39:16.840
and do more complex things than
they can do in Data Studio,

00:39:16.840 --> 00:39:20.050
without having to get down
and dirty with SQL queries.

00:39:20.050 --> 00:39:22.242
And then of course, we
have data scientists.

00:39:22.242 --> 00:39:23.200
We've got some of them.

00:39:23.200 --> 00:39:25.790
You haven't got all of
them, but we've got a few.

00:39:25.790 --> 00:39:28.150
And they are using traditional
data scientists tools.

00:39:28.150 --> 00:39:32.230
We've got Jupyter Notebooks
with Python scripts,

00:39:32.230 --> 00:39:33.550
and they are writing SQL.

00:39:33.550 --> 00:39:36.580
And then we do have a little
bit of Cloud ML and TensorFlow

00:39:36.580 --> 00:39:38.020
as well.

00:39:38.020 --> 00:39:40.690
And those guys are able to get
access to the data in the way

00:39:40.690 --> 00:39:44.426
that you've just seen
Robert demonstrate.

00:39:44.426 --> 00:39:48.320
So one final concern.

00:39:48.320 --> 00:39:52.587
I mentioned earlier the data
governance side of things.

00:39:52.587 --> 00:39:55.170
Remember, this has been going
on for the last couple of years.

00:39:55.170 --> 00:39:58.070
And if you cast your mind
back to this time last year,

00:39:58.070 --> 00:40:00.202
everybody was very
concerned about GDPR.

00:40:00.202 --> 00:40:01.910
We'd actually been,
on our side, planning

00:40:01.910 --> 00:40:04.760
for that for about 18
months before last May,

00:40:04.760 --> 00:40:07.070
making sure that
we were prepared

00:40:07.070 --> 00:40:11.945
for the additional burdens
that were going to come to us.

00:40:11.945 --> 00:40:14.070
We had a number of requirements
that the old system

00:40:14.070 --> 00:40:15.300
couldn't meet.

00:40:15.300 --> 00:40:17.850
In particular, it
was very difficult

00:40:17.850 --> 00:40:21.340
for us to respond to a
subject access request.

00:40:21.340 --> 00:40:24.120
If somebody wanted to know
from us all the data that we

00:40:24.120 --> 00:40:25.365
held on them--

00:40:25.365 --> 00:40:27.240
if you remember, it was
very, very fragmented

00:40:27.240 --> 00:40:29.790
with lots of people,
lots of data,

00:40:29.790 --> 00:40:32.130
and it was very difficult
for us to put all of that

00:40:32.130 --> 00:40:36.660
together and return
somebody a readable file

00:40:36.660 --> 00:40:39.030
with, here's all the
information we have on you.

00:40:39.030 --> 00:40:41.130
It was possible, but it
meant a lot of phone calls

00:40:41.130 --> 00:40:42.720
and CSV files being
copied around.

00:40:42.720 --> 00:40:44.650
And now we forgot
to ask Fred in IT.

00:40:47.670 --> 00:40:53.710
Also, we could opt out of things
like email marketing and so on,

00:40:53.710 --> 00:40:57.720
but we weren't easily able to
stop collecting an individual's

00:40:57.720 --> 00:40:59.220
data.

00:40:59.220 --> 00:41:00.900
And another
requirement under GDPR

00:41:00.900 --> 00:41:01.890
is that you can
collect the data,

00:41:01.890 --> 00:41:02.910
but you can't processes it.

00:41:02.910 --> 00:41:05.160
So you're not allowed to use
it for targeted marketing

00:41:05.160 --> 00:41:05.940
and so on.

00:41:05.940 --> 00:41:08.380
And that's something we
couldn't do before, either.

00:41:08.380 --> 00:41:11.340
And finally, you have
the right to have data

00:41:11.340 --> 00:41:14.220
that you can prove
is incorrect amended,

00:41:14.220 --> 00:41:16.890
or to ask that your data
is completely deleted.

00:41:16.890 --> 00:41:19.080
And both of those, we
couldn't really do.

00:41:19.080 --> 00:41:21.150
The Hadoop-based
approach we had before,

00:41:21.150 --> 00:41:23.250
the data was pretty
much write-only

00:41:23.250 --> 00:41:24.880
from our point of view.

00:41:24.880 --> 00:41:26.970
So if we had to
remove a user's data,

00:41:26.970 --> 00:41:29.590
we would have had to
copy all but that data.

00:41:29.590 --> 00:41:30.990
So you just drop the old--

00:41:30.990 --> 00:41:35.940
just cumbersome and not
really operationally feasible.

00:41:35.940 --> 00:41:40.020
So we had a bunch of challenges
that we had a legal obligation

00:41:40.020 --> 00:41:44.490
to comply with that our
old system couldn't meet.

00:41:44.490 --> 00:41:47.080
That actually was the thing
that tipped this over the edge.

00:41:47.080 --> 00:41:50.093
So the data science team
had wanted something

00:41:50.093 --> 00:41:51.510
like this in place
for a long time

00:41:51.510 --> 00:41:53.440
to make their lives easier.

00:41:53.440 --> 00:41:55.940
GDPR was the thing that finally
gave us the business driver.

00:41:55.940 --> 00:41:56.990
We're going to have
to spend some money.

00:41:56.990 --> 00:41:58.698
We're going to have
to do a bunch of work

00:41:58.698 --> 00:42:00.680
to get to where we need to be.

00:42:00.680 --> 00:42:03.410
Why not just build a system we
know that we need and we know

00:42:03.410 --> 00:42:06.255
that we want, rather than
just try and patch up

00:42:06.255 --> 00:42:07.380
the old system in some way?

00:42:07.380 --> 00:42:08.540
If we have to spend
the money anyway,

00:42:08.540 --> 00:42:10.040
why not spend it
in the right place,

00:42:10.040 --> 00:42:13.990
rather than just spending it
patching up an old system?

00:42:13.990 --> 00:42:15.720
So the new system--

00:42:15.720 --> 00:42:17.220
all the data is in one place.

00:42:17.220 --> 00:42:19.510
Implicitly, we have pointers
to where it came from.

00:42:19.510 --> 00:42:21.960
So if we do need to find
additional sources for deletion

00:42:21.960 --> 00:42:23.340
or something, we can.

00:42:23.340 --> 00:42:25.330
Remember, all the
data ends up in SUV,

00:42:25.330 --> 00:42:27.120
but that's not necessarily
the only place.

00:42:27.120 --> 00:42:31.470
There are operational databases
that will have other copies.

00:42:31.470 --> 00:42:34.977
And also, we were
able to increase

00:42:34.977 --> 00:42:37.060
the level of governance
we've got around our data.

00:42:37.060 --> 00:42:41.340
So one of the things that
we have been able to do

00:42:41.340 --> 00:42:45.420
is to separate data
which contains PII,

00:42:45.420 --> 00:42:48.780
and to provide additional
views of that data

00:42:48.780 --> 00:42:50.950
with the PII masked.

00:42:50.950 --> 00:42:53.850
Now, some people will
need the real raw PII.

00:42:53.850 --> 00:42:56.425
Imagine customer service if
someone phones up and says,

00:42:56.425 --> 00:42:58.050
hey, look I paid for
this item in game,

00:42:58.050 --> 00:42:59.795
and I didn't get
it in my inventory.

00:42:59.795 --> 00:43:01.170
Customer service
need to actually

00:43:01.170 --> 00:43:03.360
have the PII to
speak to that person

00:43:03.360 --> 00:43:04.963
and verify they
are who they are,

00:43:04.963 --> 00:43:07.380
go into their game inventory,
figure out what they've got,

00:43:07.380 --> 00:43:09.090
and resolve the problem.

00:43:09.090 --> 00:43:12.180
But most people don't
need actual customer data.

00:43:12.180 --> 00:43:13.750
They just need to
know, for example,

00:43:13.750 --> 00:43:15.600
that these players are
unique, or that they

00:43:15.600 --> 00:43:19.290
can do a join across
tables, and make sure

00:43:19.290 --> 00:43:22.110
that this gameplay data is
matched with that identity

00:43:22.110 --> 00:43:26.570
data, without knowing who
that person actually is.

00:43:26.570 --> 00:43:28.720
So the way we did
that is, I mentioned

00:43:28.720 --> 00:43:34.380
that we have two projects
within BigQuery for each game.

00:43:34.380 --> 00:43:36.630
What we have is one which
contains the raw data, which

00:43:36.630 --> 00:43:38.880
is very locked down, and to
which very few people have

00:43:38.880 --> 00:43:39.940
access.

00:43:39.940 --> 00:43:43.770
And then we have another, which
has an identical set of schema.

00:43:43.770 --> 00:43:48.900
But any PII columns
in the original data

00:43:48.900 --> 00:43:52.650
are represented by hashed
versions of that PII.

00:43:52.650 --> 00:43:56.520
So it's unique, but it's
not personally identifiable,

00:43:56.520 --> 00:43:58.562
unless you know what
that hash function is.

00:43:58.562 --> 00:44:01.020
And the hash function is rotated
every week, automatically.

00:44:01.020 --> 00:44:03.750
So from our point of view, even
if somebody takes an extract

00:44:03.750 --> 00:44:06.840
and has a snapshot of what
that mapping is, in a few days,

00:44:06.840 --> 00:44:08.470
it's going to be obsolete.

00:44:08.470 --> 00:44:09.990
So we're limiting our risk.

00:44:09.990 --> 00:44:11.070
There's not zero risk.

00:44:11.070 --> 00:44:13.333
You can't-- you have to live
with some level of risk,

00:44:13.333 --> 00:44:15.750
assuming you're not going to
completely distrust everybody

00:44:15.750 --> 00:44:17.820
in your company
to work with data.

00:44:17.820 --> 00:44:20.440
But we're able to manage
the risk appropriately.

00:44:20.440 --> 00:44:22.297
People that need the
data have access to it.

00:44:22.297 --> 00:44:23.880
People that don't
need the data, we're

00:44:23.880 --> 00:44:27.605
able to protect them, to
be honest, from having

00:44:27.605 --> 00:44:28.730
to worry too much about it.

00:44:31.580 --> 00:44:33.590
So onto the results.

00:44:33.590 --> 00:44:38.210
I'm going to talk briefly
about four experiments

00:44:38.210 --> 00:44:43.130
that we've run with the
data since we started.

00:44:43.130 --> 00:44:46.450
The first is targeting
some Facebook ads

00:44:46.450 --> 00:44:49.660
for "Shadow of the Tomb Raider."

00:44:49.660 --> 00:44:50.950
So what we did here--

00:44:50.950 --> 00:44:54.250
and this is some
phenomenal work.

00:44:54.250 --> 00:44:57.400
My team worked on the
actual segmentation

00:44:57.400 --> 00:44:59.680
and lookalike audience piece.

00:44:59.680 --> 00:45:03.280
The analytics and insights
team built the actual segments.

00:45:03.280 --> 00:45:04.780
The marketing team
at Eidos Montreal

00:45:04.780 --> 00:45:06.910
did a fantastic job
in the creative,

00:45:06.910 --> 00:45:09.220
that we'll have
look at the minute.

00:45:09.220 --> 00:45:11.060
And what we did
was we identified

00:45:11.060 --> 00:45:13.843
six segments in our--

00:45:13.843 --> 00:45:15.260
this is "Rise of
the Tomb Raider,"

00:45:15.260 --> 00:45:18.170
so the previous version of
the game-- previous iteration.

00:45:18.170 --> 00:45:21.170
We looked at the data
that we had for gameplay

00:45:21.170 --> 00:45:23.180
of "Rise of the Tomb Raider."

00:45:23.180 --> 00:45:28.560
We segmented that user base
using a number of attributes.

00:45:28.560 --> 00:45:30.700
We'll look at them in a minute.

00:45:30.700 --> 00:45:33.450
And then we had a number
of different creatives.

00:45:33.450 --> 00:45:38.040
We had six 30-second
TV spots that we used.

00:45:38.040 --> 00:45:42.070
We combined that with a bunch
of different copy lines as well,

00:45:42.070 --> 00:45:44.460
for different calls to action.

00:45:44.460 --> 00:45:47.370
And we multivariates--
it says A/B tested here.

00:45:47.370 --> 00:45:49.440
It was actually
multivariate testing

00:45:49.440 --> 00:45:53.520
to find out which creatives
resonated best with which

00:45:53.520 --> 00:45:56.720
segments of the audience.

00:45:56.720 --> 00:46:00.740
So these are, roughly
speaking, the six kinds

00:46:00.740 --> 00:46:03.480
of players that we identified.

00:46:03.480 --> 00:46:05.900
We've given them all nicknames--

00:46:05.900 --> 00:46:08.330
Veteran, Action Junkie,
Solver, Dabbler, Completionist,

00:46:08.330 --> 00:46:09.920
and Trialist.

00:46:09.920 --> 00:46:14.240
There are obviously numeric
values underneath this

00:46:14.240 --> 00:46:18.320
that we're using to produce
those segmentations.

00:46:18.320 --> 00:46:21.470
It's not a straight binary
slice in three dimensions.

00:46:21.470 --> 00:46:25.040
We've got actually a number
of different attributes here.

00:46:25.040 --> 00:46:28.027
There's a cluster
analysis process going on.

00:46:28.027 --> 00:46:29.110
Some of these are obvious.

00:46:29.110 --> 00:46:31.290
We've got things like
gameplay and hours

00:46:31.290 --> 00:46:34.320
of gameplay, percentage
completion, and things

00:46:34.320 --> 00:46:34.820
like that.

00:46:34.820 --> 00:46:35.840
Some of those, obvious--

00:46:35.840 --> 00:46:38.990
if you look at these
descriptions on screen,

00:46:38.990 --> 00:46:40.760
you can tell what
some of those were.

00:46:40.760 --> 00:46:44.780
There are others that are a
little bit more sophisticated.

00:46:44.780 --> 00:46:47.180
So for example, Action Junkie.

00:46:47.180 --> 00:46:48.860
Takes risk
frequently, attempting

00:46:48.860 --> 00:46:51.050
challenging obstacles--

00:46:51.050 --> 00:46:54.148
that's one of the measures
that contributes to that.

00:46:54.148 --> 00:46:55.190
None of these are simple.

00:46:55.190 --> 00:46:56.420
They're all kind of
combined measures.

00:46:56.420 --> 00:46:58.520
But one of the measures
that contributes to that

00:46:58.520 --> 00:47:02.420
is what the UK legal system
calls "death by misadventure."

00:47:02.420 --> 00:47:06.920
So if Lara doesn't make a jump,
or falls off a cliff edge,

00:47:06.920 --> 00:47:12.020
or drowns, or is killed
other than by an NPC,

00:47:12.020 --> 00:47:18.240
then we regard that as
adrenaline-fueled lust

00:47:18.240 --> 00:47:20.900
for challenge or something.

00:47:20.900 --> 00:47:22.960
So that's something--
the percentage

00:47:22.960 --> 00:47:27.830
death by NPC versus
environmental catastrophe

00:47:27.830 --> 00:47:29.830
is something that color
codes for Action Junkie.

00:47:29.830 --> 00:47:33.670
So you've got those
different segments there.

00:47:33.670 --> 00:47:42.720
And what we've done is the team
put together six, as I said,

00:47:42.720 --> 00:47:44.370
six 30-second spots.

00:47:44.370 --> 00:47:48.900
They're not a one-to-one
match for those six segments.

00:47:48.900 --> 00:47:50.380
There happen to be six in each.

00:47:50.380 --> 00:47:52.500
That's kind of coincidence.

00:47:52.500 --> 00:47:53.770
These spots-- I've got--

00:47:53.770 --> 00:47:55.187
I'm not going to
show all of them,

00:47:55.187 --> 00:47:58.250
but we've got a 10-second clip
for each of them at the moment

00:47:58.250 --> 00:48:00.137
that I'll show you.

00:48:00.137 --> 00:48:02.220
Things to watch out for
when you look at-- there's

00:48:02.220 --> 00:48:04.740
a little caption we can see.

00:48:04.740 --> 00:48:06.480
It says "TV Spot"
on the right here.

00:48:06.480 --> 00:48:10.440
That tells you what the
theme of that clip is.

00:48:10.440 --> 00:48:12.270
They've also got
very different music,

00:48:12.270 --> 00:48:14.290
and a different feel to them.

00:48:14.290 --> 00:48:15.870
So if we run through that--

00:48:15.870 --> 00:48:18.650
and then I'll talk a little
bit about it in a second.

00:48:18.650 --> 00:48:19.640
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:48:19.640 --> 00:48:21.130
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:48:21.130 --> 00:48:23.210
- Do you realize the
tragedy you have unleashed?

00:48:23.210 --> 00:48:24.196
- What have I done?

00:48:29.619 --> 00:48:31.591
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:48:32.577 --> 00:48:34.056
- [GASPS]

00:48:35.042 --> 00:48:36.028
[GRUNTS]

00:48:36.028 --> 00:48:37.507
[RUMBLING]

00:48:40.958 --> 00:48:42.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:48:48.353 --> 00:48:50.340
[CAMERA SHUTTER]

00:48:50.340 --> 00:48:52.500
- Because I could
not stop for death,

00:48:52.500 --> 00:48:54.231
he kindly stopped for me.

00:48:54.231 --> 00:48:54.856
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:48:54.856 --> 00:48:56.224
[CLICK]

00:48:57.136 --> 00:49:00.098
- By taking the key, you set
the Apocalypse in motion.

00:49:00.098 --> 00:49:02.054
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:49:10.367 --> 00:49:12.323
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:49:18.191 --> 00:49:21.125
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:49:21.125 --> 00:49:21.958
[END VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:49:21.958 --> 00:49:23.542
SIMON PROTHEROE: So
you can see, we've

00:49:23.542 --> 00:49:25.130
got a number of
different clips there.

00:49:25.130 --> 00:49:26.800
They've all got a very
different look and feel.

00:49:26.800 --> 00:49:28.217
Some are darker,
some are lighter,

00:49:28.217 --> 00:49:31.447
some have got more
upbeat music than others.

00:49:31.447 --> 00:49:33.280
There are some which
have dialogue and focus

00:49:33.280 --> 00:49:35.030
on the character or
the storyline-- others

00:49:35.030 --> 00:49:37.380
which focus on stunts
and things like that.

00:49:37.380 --> 00:49:40.120
And the idea was to
look at the segments we

00:49:40.120 --> 00:49:42.400
had of the different
types of data,

00:49:42.400 --> 00:49:44.740
and basically to
match those clips,

00:49:44.740 --> 00:49:47.290
and figure out which
resonated better.

00:49:47.290 --> 00:49:49.750
And then also, as
I said earlier,

00:49:49.750 --> 00:49:53.966
the copy lines that we
had on the ads as well.

00:49:53.966 --> 00:49:57.958
And give me one second.

00:49:57.958 --> 00:49:59.340
Oops.

00:49:59.340 --> 00:50:00.260
Yeah.

00:50:00.260 --> 00:50:00.930
Sorry.

00:50:00.930 --> 00:50:02.890
[CHUCKLES]

00:50:05.998 --> 00:50:07.790
It's the one thing we
couldn't get working.

00:50:11.220 --> 00:50:12.260
Thanks.

00:50:12.260 --> 00:50:15.970
So to cut straight
to the results,

00:50:15.970 --> 00:50:18.310
we found that the
segmented ads--

00:50:18.310 --> 00:50:21.550
on average, the
segmented campaigns

00:50:21.550 --> 00:50:25.353
performed twice as well as
the unsegmented campaign.

00:50:25.353 --> 00:50:26.770
So we had some
benchmark campaigns

00:50:26.770 --> 00:50:28.732
that we'd run before,
where we'd addressed

00:50:28.732 --> 00:50:30.940
pretty much the same audience
with the same creative,

00:50:30.940 --> 00:50:34.880
but with the same creative
across the whole audience.

00:50:34.880 --> 00:50:39.380
The click-through rate
was on average about

00:50:39.380 --> 00:50:43.820
literally almost exactly double
across all of these campaigns

00:50:43.820 --> 00:50:44.480
combined.

00:50:44.480 --> 00:50:46.700
Some performed even
better than that.

00:50:46.700 --> 00:50:48.530
Some a little bit
worse than double,

00:50:48.530 --> 00:50:49.710
but the average was double.

00:50:49.710 --> 00:50:51.140
So we're really happy with that.

00:50:51.140 --> 00:50:53.843
The other way of looking at it,
of course, is cost-per-click.

00:50:53.843 --> 00:50:55.760
So we've either doubled
our click-through rate

00:50:55.760 --> 00:51:00.000
or halved our marketing costs
for the same number of clicks.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:02.810
So phenomenally successful
from our point of view.

00:51:02.810 --> 00:51:05.040
Again, this is early days.

00:51:05.040 --> 00:51:06.920
But the early
indications of what

00:51:06.920 --> 00:51:09.980
we're able to do with the
data that we couldn't before

00:51:09.980 --> 00:51:11.090
have been really useful.

00:51:11.090 --> 00:51:13.790
Remember before, we
had that siloed data.

00:51:13.790 --> 00:51:16.610
We weren't able, for example,
to take the siloed data

00:51:16.610 --> 00:51:18.680
and match that to Facebook
data to build Facebook

00:51:18.680 --> 00:51:20.570
audiences, which we could now.

00:51:23.310 --> 00:51:26.780
Next, this may not look--

00:51:26.780 --> 00:51:28.780
you might not be able to
see the detail on this.

00:51:28.780 --> 00:51:33.260
But basically, this is the
"Kingdom Hearts" website.

00:51:33.260 --> 00:51:35.590
"Kingdom Hearts" came
out earlier this year,

00:51:35.590 --> 00:51:39.023
and we had the website
user journey there,

00:51:39.023 --> 00:51:40.190
which is fairly traditional.

00:51:40.190 --> 00:51:43.970
We've got a straight
landing page,

00:51:43.970 --> 00:51:46.850
and then there's a user journey.

00:51:46.850 --> 00:51:49.430
You can imagine going through
the history of the franchise,

00:51:49.430 --> 00:51:53.490
details about this particular
game, and then a Buy Now page.

00:51:53.490 --> 00:51:55.692
This was built by
the team in LA.

00:51:55.692 --> 00:51:57.150
Did a fantastic
job on the website.

00:51:57.150 --> 00:51:59.180
But what we wanted to
do is try and make sure

00:51:59.180 --> 00:52:02.640
that we were getting
maximum use out of that.

00:52:02.640 --> 00:52:06.230
And again, looking at segmenting
the audience for that.

00:52:06.230 --> 00:52:08.840
We've got sources
of data for people

00:52:08.840 --> 00:52:11.620
who were completely new
to the franchise, where

00:52:11.620 --> 00:52:14.660
starting on the landing page and
stepping through that journey

00:52:14.660 --> 00:52:16.350
is the right thing to do.

00:52:16.350 --> 00:52:18.950
We have other people who
have got a high purchase

00:52:18.950 --> 00:52:22.480
intent already, that dropping
them straight into the Buy page

00:52:22.480 --> 00:52:24.007
is the right thing to do.

00:52:24.007 --> 00:52:26.090
But equally, if you get
that the wrong way around,

00:52:26.090 --> 00:52:27.500
the last thing you
want to do is take

00:52:27.500 --> 00:52:29.330
somebody who's ready
to buy and bore them

00:52:29.330 --> 00:52:31.622
by taking them through a
whole history of the franchise

00:52:31.622 --> 00:52:32.670
that they already know.

00:52:32.670 --> 00:52:36.080
Or for somebody
who doesn't really

00:52:36.080 --> 00:52:38.900
know much that has just
heard about this Pixar thing

00:52:38.900 --> 00:52:40.490
that we've done, and wants to--

00:52:40.490 --> 00:52:42.955
maybe they've picked
up some marketing

00:52:42.955 --> 00:52:45.080
on the Disney side of
things, and doesn't know much

00:52:45.080 --> 00:52:47.940
about the game--

00:52:47.940 --> 00:52:51.240
dropping them straight in a Buy
Now page feels really abrupt.

00:52:51.240 --> 00:52:54.540
So what we did was look at
the sources of the traffic,

00:52:54.540 --> 00:52:59.910
analyze previous root
results from those sources,

00:52:59.910 --> 00:53:02.640
and then have the--

00:53:02.640 --> 00:53:05.970
basically, the consumer
dropped into that user journey

00:53:05.970 --> 00:53:09.432
from those ads at what we felt
was the right place for them.

00:53:09.432 --> 00:53:11.640
And this performed even
better than the "Tomb Raider"

00:53:11.640 --> 00:53:12.140
campaign.

00:53:12.140 --> 00:53:16.560
We got click-through rates
increased by 144% here.

00:53:16.560 --> 00:53:18.720
Click-through is
to retailer links.

00:53:18.720 --> 00:53:21.970
And overall conversion
increased by 20%.

00:53:21.970 --> 00:53:26.970
So again, really happy with
the results from that site.

00:53:26.970 --> 00:53:29.470
A couple of others that I'll
touch on very briefly before we

00:53:29.470 --> 00:53:32.890
talk about next steps.

00:53:32.890 --> 00:53:35.470
With "Life is Strange 2,"
which is an episodic game,

00:53:35.470 --> 00:53:37.450
we localized into a
lot more languages

00:53:37.450 --> 00:53:39.070
than we did for
"Life is Strange 1."

00:53:39.070 --> 00:53:41.260
"Life is Strange
2," we saw that we

00:53:41.260 --> 00:53:43.690
had users in new markets
like Russia and so

00:53:43.690 --> 00:53:46.910
on, but the game itself wasn't
localized into those languages.

00:53:46.910 --> 00:53:49.650
So for "2," we localized
into those languages.

00:53:49.650 --> 00:53:51.370
And what we wanted
to do is measure

00:53:51.370 --> 00:53:54.560
the effectiveness of that.

00:53:54.560 --> 00:53:57.122
We can't necessarily just
look at sales data and say,

00:53:57.122 --> 00:53:58.580
we sold double the
number of units.

00:53:58.580 --> 00:54:02.030
We need to look at other
data as well, user the data,

00:54:02.030 --> 00:54:04.105
where people are
playing the game,

00:54:04.105 --> 00:54:06.230
correlating that with
purchase-- because obviously,

00:54:06.230 --> 00:54:10.880
they will be in different
markets, other than--

00:54:10.880 --> 00:54:14.105
there are sources of
gameplay other than people

00:54:14.105 --> 00:54:15.230
just buying their own copy.

00:54:15.230 --> 00:54:17.000
There's lots of
second-hand, there's

00:54:17.000 --> 00:54:18.650
piracy, and so on as well.

00:54:18.650 --> 00:54:22.670
So understanding that
helped us really figure out

00:54:22.670 --> 00:54:26.870
how we were going to
optimize our localization

00:54:26.870 --> 00:54:29.160
spends in future.

00:54:29.160 --> 00:54:31.580
So having done
this, what we found

00:54:31.580 --> 00:54:34.220
is that that
localization effort,

00:54:34.220 --> 00:54:36.470
having analyzed the data,
using the data in BigQuery,

00:54:36.470 --> 00:54:38.660
we can find that we've
got, in some countries,

00:54:38.660 --> 00:54:41.390
double the share of
users for this version

00:54:41.390 --> 00:54:42.600
than we had previously.

00:54:42.600 --> 00:54:44.780
So that's share, not
absolute numbers.

00:54:44.780 --> 00:54:48.950
So the indication there is that
localizing to those languages

00:54:48.950 --> 00:54:50.930
has really had an
impact on increasing

00:54:50.930 --> 00:54:54.020
take-up in those countries.

00:54:54.020 --> 00:54:57.918
And finally, timing
analysis for episodic games.

00:54:57.918 --> 00:54:59.960
This is something that
we're doing at the moment.

00:54:59.960 --> 00:55:00.770
The work's ongoing.

00:55:00.770 --> 00:55:03.290
But what we're trying to do
is look at improving our sales

00:55:03.290 --> 00:55:06.230
forecasting, and
eventually get to the point

00:55:06.230 --> 00:55:11.600
where we can actually control
our sales curve to some extent

00:55:11.600 --> 00:55:15.050
by affecting the timing
of episodic releases.

00:55:15.050 --> 00:55:16.610
For some games,
some players will

00:55:16.610 --> 00:55:22.340
be super excited about a
game, and pre-order a release.

00:55:22.340 --> 00:55:24.857
For other games, they may
play the first episode,

00:55:24.857 --> 00:55:26.690
and then buy a season
pass to enjoy the rest

00:55:26.690 --> 00:55:27.650
once they've bought in.

00:55:27.650 --> 00:55:31.930
They're treating the
first episode as a trial.

00:55:31.930 --> 00:55:34.580
Player C there--
maybe those people

00:55:34.580 --> 00:55:37.250
are buying the collector's
edition at the end.

00:55:37.250 --> 00:55:39.500
Or perhaps they-- maybe
they've played the whole game,

00:55:39.500 --> 00:55:41.083
maybe they haven't,
but they're buying

00:55:41.083 --> 00:55:43.010
much later in the sequence.

00:55:43.010 --> 00:55:45.320
But then you've got
potentially Player D,

00:55:45.320 --> 00:55:48.860
which is a group we didn't
get at all, who maybe played

00:55:48.860 --> 00:55:52.610
the initial free first episode,
and then just dropped out

00:55:52.610 --> 00:55:53.900
of the process entirely.

00:55:53.900 --> 00:55:57.560
So understanding
what that looks like,

00:55:57.560 --> 00:56:01.970
how those player
bases segment out,

00:56:01.970 --> 00:56:03.730
and how the timing
of those releases

00:56:03.730 --> 00:56:06.230
affects that is something we're
doing at the moment in order

00:56:06.230 --> 00:56:08.930
to improve not only our
forecasting in the short term,

00:56:08.930 --> 00:56:11.810
but long term, the ability to
actually control that sales

00:56:11.810 --> 00:56:13.126
pipeline.

00:56:13.126 --> 00:56:16.640
So some basic examples there.

00:56:16.640 --> 00:56:20.288
I think as we've
said, we've now got

00:56:20.288 --> 00:56:22.830
to a situation where there's a
lot more experimentation going

00:56:22.830 --> 00:56:23.010
on.

00:56:23.010 --> 00:56:25.177
A lot of those constraints
that we mentioned earlier

00:56:25.177 --> 00:56:26.220
have gone away.

00:56:26.220 --> 00:56:27.960
We've been really
happy with the results

00:56:27.960 --> 00:56:29.335
that we've got
from the platform.

00:56:29.335 --> 00:56:32.417
We've got a really solid
relationship with the GCP team

00:56:32.417 --> 00:56:34.500
where we feel we're getting
a lot of support where

00:56:34.500 --> 00:56:37.650
we need it, and fantastic
tech to build on.

00:56:40.260 --> 00:56:44.850
We have worked on a number of
the challenges that we had.

00:56:44.850 --> 00:56:47.060
For example, the
Schema migration,

00:56:47.060 --> 00:56:49.140
and got great results there.

00:56:49.140 --> 00:56:51.382
We have a happy, smiling DPO.

00:56:51.382 --> 00:56:54.050
I would have shown you a
picture, but that's PII

00:56:54.050 --> 00:56:57.320
and I'm not allowed to do it
without written consent, so--

00:56:57.320 --> 00:56:59.750
But we're comfortable now.

00:56:59.750 --> 00:57:02.480
We're a lot more secure than
we were in our governance

00:57:02.480 --> 00:57:04.130
around the data.

00:57:04.130 --> 00:57:06.470
And this is the first
real cloud native

00:57:06.470 --> 00:57:10.640
application that we've
built. We had infrastructure

00:57:10.640 --> 00:57:11.900
in public cloud before.

00:57:11.900 --> 00:57:14.300
We had web servers
deployed on Kubernetes

00:57:14.300 --> 00:57:17.790
in different environments.

00:57:17.790 --> 00:57:19.970
But we hadn't
really taken a leap

00:57:19.970 --> 00:57:22.330
and built anything cloud native.

00:57:22.330 --> 00:57:24.780
What we were doing really is
infrastructure as a service,

00:57:24.780 --> 00:57:29.570
so it was effectively just not
paying for the hardware CapEx.

00:57:29.570 --> 00:57:32.570
But this is the first time that
we've really got into that,

00:57:32.570 --> 00:57:36.068
and it's been
incredibly successful.

00:57:36.068 --> 00:57:38.360
So what's next for us in
terms of the single-gamer view

00:57:38.360 --> 00:57:39.650
platform?

00:57:39.650 --> 00:57:42.140
Well, to torture
the Pokemon analogy,

00:57:42.140 --> 00:57:44.030
we've got some
legendary data sources

00:57:44.030 --> 00:57:46.550
that we still want to catch.

00:57:46.550 --> 00:57:50.510
There are some games which
aren't yet in this system,

00:57:50.510 --> 00:57:52.750
for various reasons, and we're--

00:57:52.750 --> 00:57:55.400
having got these
initial proof points,

00:57:55.400 --> 00:57:58.070
we're now able to bring
some of those data sources

00:57:58.070 --> 00:58:00.710
in and open up
those conversations.

00:58:00.710 --> 00:58:04.040
A year in, we've also
got some good use cases

00:58:04.040 --> 00:58:08.480
where we can justify automating
or building further tooling

00:58:08.480 --> 00:58:09.830
around the platform.

00:58:09.830 --> 00:58:13.520
So things like we mentioned
earlier, the ability

00:58:13.520 --> 00:58:18.320
to filter users and to either
capture or not capture data,

00:58:18.320 --> 00:58:22.520
or to process or not process
data, and having that process

00:58:22.520 --> 00:58:23.480
automated.

00:58:23.480 --> 00:58:26.813
The API-driven is something that
we're working on at the moment.

00:58:26.813 --> 00:58:28.730
We're looking at a
cross-platform segmentation

00:58:28.730 --> 00:58:30.470
framework, so the
sort of segmentation

00:58:30.470 --> 00:58:32.033
that we did for
"Tomb Raider," we're

00:58:32.033 --> 00:58:33.200
able to have those segments.

00:58:33.200 --> 00:58:35.810
At the moment, those segments
are extracted out, and then

00:58:35.810 --> 00:58:40.550
used to build audience lists
for Facebook and so on.

00:58:40.550 --> 00:58:42.920
With the cross-platform
segmentation framework,

00:58:42.920 --> 00:58:45.800
what we're intending to do is
have the segmentations native

00:58:45.800 --> 00:58:48.680
to the system itself so
that we can pass them

00:58:48.680 --> 00:58:52.680
around as first-class objects
within the architecture.

00:58:52.680 --> 00:58:55.700
And finally, all the
examples I've given here

00:58:55.700 --> 00:58:59.810
have been marketing related, or
maybe sales analysis related.

00:58:59.810 --> 00:59:01.570
The reason for that is twofold.

00:59:01.570 --> 00:59:04.100
First of all, my team
is responsible for most

00:59:04.100 --> 00:59:05.910
of those websites
and stores and so on.

00:59:05.910 --> 00:59:10.190
So it's within our team's
ability to do those controls.

00:59:10.190 --> 00:59:12.070
We work very closely
with the analytics team.

00:59:12.070 --> 00:59:14.570
And between the two of us, we're
able to do this without too

00:59:14.570 --> 00:59:16.333
much additional buy in.

00:59:16.333 --> 00:59:17.750
But the other
thing, obviously, is

00:59:17.750 --> 00:59:20.000
that the dev cycle on
a marketing campaign

00:59:20.000 --> 00:59:22.450
is weeks or months at most.

00:59:22.450 --> 00:59:25.560
The dev cycle on a game
is months or years.

00:59:25.560 --> 00:59:28.400
And so it was going to
be much quicker for us

00:59:28.400 --> 00:59:30.800
to get those kind
of proof points

00:59:30.800 --> 00:59:32.450
by working on
marketing campaigns

00:59:32.450 --> 00:59:34.675
to show the idea--
the proof of concept

00:59:34.675 --> 00:59:38.640
of what we want to do with
user data and segmentation.

00:59:38.640 --> 00:59:41.000
But the next step for us
is then closing that loop,

00:59:41.000 --> 00:59:42.830
and starting to use
that data from BigQuery

00:59:42.830 --> 00:59:45.380
natively in games to
do personalization

00:59:45.380 --> 00:59:46.760
and so on within
the game itself,

00:59:46.760 --> 00:59:49.813
rather than outside the game.

00:59:49.813 --> 00:59:50.480
And what's next?

00:59:50.480 --> 00:59:52.705
So that was what was next
for the SGV platform.

00:59:52.705 --> 00:59:54.330
What's next for Square
Enix in general?

00:59:54.330 --> 00:59:58.710
Well, we are deploying
game servers on GCP

00:59:58.710 --> 01:00:01.664
for some multiplayer
titles coming up,

01:00:01.664 --> 01:00:03.512
which is going to
generate a lot of data.

01:00:06.750 --> 01:00:09.810
We are also deploying a lot
of the back-end services,

01:00:09.810 --> 01:00:12.270
so our identity systems,
billing systems,

01:00:12.270 --> 01:00:16.467
store sales, and so on are
being deployed in GCP as well.

01:00:16.467 --> 01:00:18.300
Again, it's going to
generate a lot of data.

01:00:21.160 --> 01:00:23.580
And finally, we're scaling
up analytics pipeline,

01:00:23.580 --> 01:00:25.920
the SGV platform itself, to
be able to cope with much

01:00:25.920 --> 01:00:27.690
larger portions of data.

01:00:27.690 --> 01:00:29.190
Fortunately, of
those three pillars,

01:00:29.190 --> 01:00:30.898
that's the easiest
for us, because that's

01:00:30.898 --> 01:00:33.850
something that pretty much is
handled entirely by Google.

01:00:33.850 --> 01:00:35.550
So the scaling side
of it is something

01:00:35.550 --> 01:00:37.623
that is no longer a
big challenge for us.

01:00:37.623 --> 01:00:39.540
We just have to write a
slightly bigger check.

01:00:39.540 --> 01:00:40.040
That's fine.

01:00:42.370 --> 01:00:43.537
And that's it.

01:00:43.537 --> 01:00:45.370
I don't think we'll
have time for questions.

01:00:45.370 --> 01:00:47.170
I've left my email
address there.

01:00:47.170 --> 01:00:49.420
Feel free to get in touch
if you've got any questions,

01:00:49.420 --> 01:00:51.490
or come up afterwards.

01:00:51.490 --> 01:00:53.000
But otherwise,
thank you very much.

01:00:53.000 --> 01:00:55.750
[APPLAUSE]

